{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"**Patchset 2 pushed** to Gerrit change 2658 (`939dab422518`), rebased onto current `origin/main` (`084e753d30df`). Change-Id preserved.\n\nAddresses the `iac` advisory on `.github/workflows/terraform-drift.yml`: `id-token: write` moved from the workflow top level onto the `plan` job that actually exchanges it.\n\nI checked the convention before changing it, as asked. Every other workflow here that assumes an OIDC role scopes the grant to the job: `external-integration.yml` (two jobs), `reconcile-bridge.yml`, `structured-output-baseline.yml`, `release.yml`. `terraform-drift.yml` was the sole outlier. `release.yml` goes further and depends on that scoping for a security property — its `mcp_verify` job deliberately holds no `id-token` so an unverified binary can never run under the publishing identity.\n\nWorth noting this advisory got sharper because of my own change: the top-level grant now also handed the token to the new `fmt` job, which makes no AWS call at all. Splitting the jobs turned a workflow-wide grant into a genuinely over-broad one.\n\nThe top-level `permissions:` block is removed entirely rather than trimmed to `contents: read`, since both jobs now declare their own explicit blocks (`fmt`: `contents: read`; `plan`: `contents: read` + `id-token: write`).\n\nPinned by `test_oidc_token_is_scoped_to_the_job_that_exchanges_it` in `tests/unit/test_terraform_drift_workflow_shape.py`, which asserts three things: no workflow-level `id-token`, the plan job declares it, and **no other job holds it** — so a future job cannot inherit or acquire the token without tripping the test. RED against patchset 1, verified in a throwaway worktree at the old commit.\n\nLocal state: `make lint` green (complexity `new=0 increased=0`; mechanism `new=0 increased=0 stale=0`), `make typecheck` green, terraform/drift/mechanism/workflow selection 1395 passed. One unrelated failure appeared in that run — `tests/unit/workflow/test_gate_error_sidecar.py` raised `GateCongestedError: all 4 concurrent slots busy`, a gate-admission collision with the two `review-plan` runs in flight elsewhere. It passes on its own re-run; it touches nothing in this change and is not a defect I introduced."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788587082688087517,"uuid":"01fe23bb-61b7-4fce-a19b-181622199db7"}